// 0014-trellis-whitespace-optional.peg — PROTOTYPE grammar exploring
// whitespace-insignificant trellis (FDR 0022 Amendment,
// "Whitespace-insignificance via identifier-limiting", 2026-07-19).
//
// This is a COPY of docs/rfcs/0014-trellis.peg with a targeted restructuring
// that makes whitespace OPTIONAL around combinators and inside subpaths,
// without changing the meaning of any well-spaced query. It is not (yet) the
// normative grammar; 0014-trellis.peg remains authoritative. The amendment
// in docs/features/0022-trellis.md records the reasoning, the cost, and the
// residue this prototype deliberately does NOT resolve.
//
// The delta from the normative grammar, in one sentence: disambiguation is
// pushed into the IDENTIFIER class at TOKEN granularity — an identifier
// terminal may not be the `-` that BEGINS a combinator token (`->`, `->>`,
// `-[`) — so `a->b` self-delimits into `a` / `->` / `b` with no space, and
// the required `SP` around combinators relaxes to optional `SP?`.
//
// WHAT THIS BUYS (validated against the conformance vectors below, all of
// which still parse, PLUS the new glued forms):
//   - `a->b` == `a -> b` (was: FieldPred `a-` `>` `b`; now: traversal)
//   - `[->x]`, `[+x]` == `[-> x]`, `[+ x]` (the latter WAS required)
//   - every well-spaced query keeps its exact meaning
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY (the documented residue — see the FDR amendment):
//   1. Two bare identifiers still require a separating space: `todo urgent`
//      is two terms, `todourgent` is one. This is INTRINSIC (IdentRune
//      excludes SP1) and unavoidable under exclusion-class identifiers; it
//      is not a "whitespace rule", it is tokenization. Step/ConjRun keep the
//      REQUIRED `SP` term-separator precisely so a missing space between two
//      bare identifiers/values stays a loud error rather than silently
//      re-lexing (`year=2026 feed=hn` must keep its space; glued it would
//      mis-parse as `year=2026feed` + `=hn`).
//   2. The term-final SIGIL SUFFIX is boundary-defined by construction:
//      `todo:` (tag + latest sigil) vs `todo:x` (one identifier) differ by
//      the following rune, so `x :` and `x:` cannot be unified without
//      abolishing colon-bearing opaque identifiers (`caldav:fastmail`).
//   3. The BACKWARD/COMPARISON collision is NOT resolved here: `a<-b` still
//      parses as FieldPred `a` `<` `-b`, because the `<` is consumed by the
//      field-operator layer BEFORE Path's combinator loop runs. Forcing
//      `<-`/`<<-` to win when glued needs a FieldOp gate (`'<' !'-'`) whose
//      cost is DELETING the "less-than a dash-led bareword value" spelling
//      (`rank<-3` could no longer mean `rank < -3`; it would require
//      quoting, `rank<"-3"`). That is a language-level policy decision left
//      to the amendment's reviewers, not silently baked in.
//
// Notation: Ford PEG, authored langlang-compatible (FDR 0022): // comments
// only, explicit spacing. As with the normative grammar, feed to langlang
// with builtin spacing DISABLED (`-disable-builtins -disable-spaces`): the
// point of this prototype is that whitespace-insignificance is achieved by
// the GRAMMAR's own (now-optional) SP tokens and self-delimiting identifier
// class, NOT by langlang's inject-optional-space-everywhere handler, which
// remains the wrong tool (it is optional at EVERY sequence boundary,
// including the intrinsic bare-identifier separator in point 1).

// ---- entry ---------------------------------------------------------------

Query        <- SP? Path SP? EOF

// A path is steps joined by combinators. The result set is the last
// step's (object, version) pairs. Whitespace around combinators is now
// OPTIONAL (`SP?`): the combinator tokens self-delimit against the
// identifier class (see IdentRune's `-` guard) and against field
// operators on their own left edge, so `a->b`, `a-> b`, `a ->b`, and
// `a -> b` all parse identically as the traversal. A LEADING combinator
// (optional prefix) is a traversal from the implicit DEFAULT ANCHOR — the
// root aggregate, FDR 0022 "roots as nodes" — so `->>!task ^done` means
// "from all roots, walk containment to undone tasks." PEG ordered choice
// consumes the prefix greedily; a leading dependent-tag term like
// `-content` matches no Combinator (its `-` is followed by `c`, not `>`),
// so the prefix falls through to empty and Step parses it.
Path         <- (Combinator SP?)? Step (SP? Combinator SP? Step)*

// A step is a maximal run of space-separated terms, ANDed, all
// predicating the same (object, version) pair. The term separator stays
// REQUIRED `SP` (intrinsic, point 1 above). The !Combinator guard is
// what ends a step: `-` may begin an identifier (dependent tags), but
// `->` after whitespace is always a combinator.
Step         <- Term (SP !Combinator Term)*

// ---- terms (per-step predicate layer, doddish-inherited) -----------------

// `^` negates the next atom or group; `=` forces exact (non-prefix)
// matching. Both apply to the term they prefix, per doddish.
Term         <- '^'? '='? BasicTerm

// FieldPred is tried before the identifier alternatives so `"name"=v`
// reads as a field predicate; a bare String (no operator) falls through
// to QuotedRef — a quoted identifier, the opaque-reference escape hatch
// for content containing reserved runes ("one/uno.zettel", a URI with a
// query string).
BasicTerm    <- Group
              / FieldPred
              / TypeTerm Sigil?
              / DigestTerm Sigil?
              / MarklTerm Sigil?     // before QuotedRef/Ident: `a@b` and `"a b"@c`
              / QuotedRef Sigil?     // are markl terms, not identifier + dangling @
              / Ident Sigil?
              / Sigil                 // bare sigil: empty predicate (doddish `:`)
QuotedRef    <- String

TypeTerm     <- '!' Ident             // type identity (not genre; dodder FDR 0018)
DigestTerm   <- '@' Ident             // blob identity — content-addressed exact match

// Sigils choose the step's candidate version-set and are combinable
// (`:.`). A sigil on any term — or bare — scopes the WHOLE step (pair
// model): some single version satisfies every term of the step.
// Per-term version binding is the version subpath, below.
//   :  latest (default)   +  history / captured revisions
//   .  external / local materializations   ?  hidden (host-defined)
Sigil        <- SigilRune+
SigilRune    <- [:+.?]

// ---- groups --------------------------------------------------------------

// One bracket syntax, three readings, disambiguated by the first token:
//   combinator  => spatial subpath predicate  [-> ...]
//   sigil       => version subpath predicate  [+ ...]
//   otherwise   => OR-alternatives            [a b, c]
// Subpaths are existential and leave the subject unchanged; each subpath
// is independently quantified. OR-alternatives are comma-separated
// CONJUNCTION RUNS (extends doddish's single-atom alternatives) — load-
// bearing for isometry: every box-format interior is a one-alternative
// group, so a bare step and its bracketed spelling are equivalent.
Group        <- '[' SP? GroupBody SP? ']'
GroupBody    <- SubPath / VersionSub / Alternatives

// [-> ...] full paths allowed inside (chained hops, closures, nesting).
// [->] (empty) means "has any outgoing edge", mirroring [+]. The space
// between the leading combinator and its path is now OPTIONAL (`SP?`), so
// `[->x]` == `[-> x]`.
SubPath      <- Combinator (SP? Path)?

// [+ ...] existential over this object's versions; v1 restricts the
// content to a step (no combinators inside — deferred). [+] (empty)
// means "has at least one recorded revision"; ^[+] its negation. The
// space between the sigil and its step is now OPTIONAL (`SP?`), so
// `[+x]` == `[+ x]` (the latter WAS required — `[+x]` previously failed,
// the sigil greedily consuming `+` and stranding `x`).
VersionSub   <- Sigil (SP? Step)?

Alternatives <- ConjRun (SP? ',' SP? ConjRun)*
ConjRun      <- Term (SP !Combinator Term)*

// ---- field predicates ----------------------------------------------------

// Flat per-type field namespace; the plugin owns the flattening (facet
// keys are field projections in the same namespace). Quoted field names
// are OPAQUE — "event.summary" names a field; dots are characters, not
// paths (arrows walk; names name). The `_` prefix is reserved for
// framework virtual fields; the reservation applies to the unquoted
// content and to field-name position only.
FieldPred    <- FieldName SP? FieldOp SP? (ValueList / Value)
FieldName    <- String / Ident

// Longest-match first. `~=` is RESERVED (regex, deferred): parsed here,
// rejected at validation.
FieldOp      <- '*=' / '^=' / '$=' / '!=' / '<=' / '>=' / '~=' / '=' / '<' / '>'

// Value lists distribute the operator as OR:
//   k*=[v1, v2]  ==  [k*=v1, k*=v2]
// DigestTerm is admitted as a value (amended 2026-07-18, surfaced by
// hyphence RFC 0002's isometry check): digest-valued field predicates
// are the query-side spelling of reference-valued fields / typed edges
// (`_mother=@blake2b256-…`, `_base=@…`) and of edge-target matching.
ValueList    <- '[' SP? Value (SP? ',' SP? Value)* SP? ']'
Value        <- MarklTerm / String / DigestTerm / Bareword

// ---- combinators: typed-arrow traversal ----------------------------------

// Every edge has a type; containment (parent->child) is the built-in
// edge type every traversal plugin provides. Edge brackets hold a
// compound predicate (ConjRun — no traversal inside, v1) evaluated
// against the EDGE; a bare name is the degenerate identifier predicate.
// Closures (`->>`, `<<-`) are one-or-more hops with visited-set
// deduplication — termination is defined on cyclic graphs.
// Longest-match first: closure forms before their single-hop prefixes.
// TypedClosure (`-[pred]->>`) is RESERVED: parsed, rejected at
// validation until hosts serve typed edges (walkthrough #6).
Combinator   <- TypedClosure / TypedFwd / TypedBack
              / '->>' / '<<-'
              / '->'  / '<-'
TypedClosure <- '-[' SP? ConjRun SP? ']->>'
TypedFwd     <- '-[' SP? ConjRun SP? ']->'
TypedBack    <- '<-[' SP? ConjRun SP? ']-'

// ---- lexical -------------------------------------------------------------

// Identifiers are opaque object references using rune-class EXCLUSION,
// matching doddish's scanner discipline: everything that is not
// whitespace and not a reserved delimiter is identifier. `-` and `/` are
// admitted inline (`area-personal`, `one/uno`, leading `-` for dependent
// tags).
//
// THE STRICT SIGIL RULE: sigil runes are identifier-INTERIOR unless the
// run is term-final — a SigilRune continues an identifier only when
// another identifier rune follows (the &IdentRune lookahead). So
// `caldav:fastmail`, `web:http://example.com`, and `12.7` are single
// identifiers, while `todo:` is identifier + sigil.
//
// THE `-` TOKEN GUARD (this prototype's delta): a `-` is identifier
// content EXCEPT when it begins a combinator token — i.e. when it is
// immediately followed by `>` (`->`, `->>`) or `[` (`-[`, the typed-edge
// forms). This is the whole mechanism that lets `a->b` self-delimit into
// `a` / `->` / `b`. It is stated on the identifier side, not the operator
// side (RFC 0014 keeps combinators pristine). Because `-` is not in the
// Reserved set, it would otherwise be re-admitted by the final
// exclusion alternative, so that alternative also excludes it (`!'-'`),
// leaving the guarded branch as the ONLY way `-` becomes identifier
// content. COST: a tag whose literal content contains `->` or `-[` (rare)
// must now be quoted (`"a->b"`); a bare hyphen elsewhere is unaffected
// (`area-personal`, `-content`, `foo-`).
//
// MARKL IDS ARE STRUCTURED TWO-SLOT TERMS (unchanged from the normative
// grammar): a purpose-full markl id decomposes as MarklTerm — a purpose
// slot (bare identifier or quoted string) joined by `@` to a digest slot.
MarklTerm    <- (String / Ident) '@' Ident
Ident        <- IdentRune+
IdentRune    <- ('-' !'>' !'[') / '/' / (SigilRune &IdentRune) / (!Reserved !'-' !SP1 .)
Reserved     <- [\[\]^=,!@<>*$~%#"'] / SigilRune

// Doddish quoting rules verbatim: double or single quotes; backslash
// escapes \n \t \r \a \b \f \v, \" and \\ round-trip, unknown escapes
// pass the following character through unchanged.
String       <- '"' (!'"' Char)* '"'
              / "'" (!"'" Char)* "'"
Char         <- '\\' . / .
Bareword     <- IdentRune+

SP           <- SP1+
SP1          <- [ \t\r\n]
EOF          <- !.

// ---- conformance vectors -------------------------------------------------
//
// All of the RFC 0014 vectors below MUST still parse with UNCHANGED
// meaning (verified by tools/validate against this grammar). The
// whitespace-optional forms are ADDITIONS, not replacements.
//
//   !task priority-1_should [due<="2026-08-01"]
//   caldav:fastmail -> component=VEVENT dtstart^="20260718"
//   web:http://example.com +
//   12.7
//   _mother=@blake2b256-9ft3x
//   piggy-piv_auth-v1@ssh_ecdsa_nistp256_pub-qqxyz
//   one/uno@blake2b256-9ft3x
//   "my thing"@blake2b256-9ft3x
//   due = "2026-08-01"
//   !task ^done project-cutting-garden
//   ->> !task ^done
//   -[blocks]->> !task ^done
//   !web-page-v1 [+]
//   !web-page-v1 ^[+]
//   !forgejo-issue-v1 state=open [+ state=closed]
//   [story-8841 !newsblur-story-v1 year=2026 [-> content-8841 @blake2b256-9ft3x]]
//
// NEW whitespace-optional forms this prototype accepts (each equivalent
// to its spaced sibling above). Note the boundary this optionality
// applies to: a COMBINATOR boundary (Path level) or a SUBPATH-HEAD
// boundary (combinator/sigil to its inner path/step). It does NOT apply
// to a term-AND boundary — see the second list.
//
//   a->b                 — traversal (was: FieldPred `a-` `>` `b`)
//   !task->!done         — `!task -> !done` (was: a syntax error)
//   ->>!task ^done       — leading combinator, no following space
//   caldav:fastmail->component=VEVENT
//   [->content-8841]     — spatial subpath, combinator glued to its path
//   [+state=closed]      — version subpath, sigil glued to its step
//
// STILL REQUIRING their space (term-AND boundary, point 1 — a group or
// prefixed term following another TERM is the intrinsic separator, kept
// required so bare-value adjacency stays a loud error, not a silent
// re-lex). These are NOT accepted glued:
//
//   !web-page-v1 [+]     — group after a term: the space is required
//   !task ^done          — two terms: the space is required
//   year=2026 feed=hn    — two field predicates: glued would mis-lex as
//                          `year=2026feed` + `=hn`, so the space is required
//
// UNCHANGED negative cases (still syntax errors):
//
//   ->>                  — leading combinator with no following step
//   done@                — dangling MarklTerm digest slot
//   "unterminated        — unterminated string
//   []                   — empty group
//
// CHANGED negative case (now VALID under this prototype):
//
//   !task->!done         — was rejected (no FieldPred escape for `!task`);
//                          now a legal traversal `!task -> !done`. This is
//                          the intended consequence of glued combinators.
